.TH ANONIFY 1 "2026-11-06" "anonify 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
anonify \- temporary, reversible network privacy hardening
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B anonify
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B anonify
applies network-anonymity hardening to a Linux machine and can take it back
off again. It randomizes the MAC address and hostname, applies a set of
kernel hardening keys, closes IPv6 and DNS leaks, brings up Tor or WireGuard
behind a fail-closed
.BR nftables
kill switch, silences LAN discovery, audits the result and can run individual
applications in a sandbox.
.PP
All of its own state lives in
.I /run/anonify
(tmpfs) and in kernel runtime state, so
.B anonify restore
or a reboot returns the machine to its original configuration. It never
deletes, alters or suppresses logs, audit records, shell or browser
histories, timestamps or evidence, and it does not hide activity from the
system's own accounting.
.PP
Commands that change the system must run as root. Only
.BR doctor " and " launch
work without it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-lang \fR(\fIen\fR|\fIes\fR)
Force a message language instead of detecting it from the OS locale.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress the startup banner and decorative output.
.TP
.B \-\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR
Safety net: run
.B restore
automatically after the given number of seconds no matter what. Recommended
while testing modules that can cut your own connectivity (tor, vpn, dns,
killswitch).
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B enable \fIMODULE\fR...
Enable one or more modules. Vetted layers are enabled in a safe order, and a
failure rolls back what was already applied. Without arguments it lists the
available modules; with
.B \-\-all
it enables mac, hostname, sysctl, ipv6, tor and dns.
.TP
.B disable \fR[\fIMODULE\fR...]
Disable the given modules, or every active module when called without
arguments.
.TP
.B restore
Revert every change recorded in the state file, verifying each value by
reading the real system state. Original values are only discarded once the
restore has been confirmed.
.TP
.B rotate
Re-randomize the MAC addresses and hostname of the active
.B mac
and
.B hostname
modules without touching the saved originals, so
.B restore
still returns the machine to its real values.
.TP
.B status
Show active modules, the values applied and any drift from the recorded
state.
.TP
.B check
Run fail-closed leak checks (DNS, IPv6, ICMP, routes, Tor/VPN, kill switch)
without sending clear-net requests when no anonymity layer is active.
.TP
.B doctor
Read-only preflight: verifies dependencies, privileges, state integrity and
environment hazards before anything is enabled. Works without root.
.B \-\-json
emits a stable, machine-readable report.
.TP
.B watch
Watch for configuration drift and optionally restore on the first hit.
.TP
.B launch \fISUBCOMMAND\fR
Run an application with a sanitized environment and, optionally, in a
.BR bubblewrap
sandbox. The timezone and locale are set for that process only; the system
clock is never touched. Subcommands:
.BR vbox ,
.BR proton ,
.B browser
and
.BR exec .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/anonify/state.json
Active modules and the original values needed for rollback. Permissions 0600,
written atomically, and removed by a successful
.BR restore .
.SH EXAMPLES
.LP
Check the machine before touching anything:
.PP
.RS 4
anonify doctor
.RE
.PP
Enable the core layers with a safety net that restores after ten minutes:
.PP
.RS 4
sudo anonify \-\-timeout 600 enable \-\-all
.RE
.PP
Put everything back:
.PP
.RS 4
sudo anonify restore
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR anonify-browser (1),
.BR nft (8),
.BR tor (1),
.BR wg-quick (8)
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B anonify
reduces what a network observer can correlate; it does not provide absolute
anonymity, and it does not protect an already compromised machine, nor
identities the user authenticates with on purpose. See the project
documentation for the full threat model.
